v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 162\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Computation and Language\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 22 Nov 2025]\nL9: # Title:PPoGA: Predictive Plan-on-Graph with Action for Knowledge Graph Question Answering\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite5†MinGyu Jeon , cite6†SuWan Cho , cite7†JaeYoung Shu L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled PPoGA: Predictive Plan-on-Graph with Action for Knowledge Graph Question Answering, by MinGyu Jeon and 2 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite8†View PDF cite9†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Large Language Models (LLMs) augmented with Knowledge Graphs (KGs) have advanced complex question answering, yet they often remain susceptible to failure when their initial high-level reasoning plan is flawed. This limitation, analogous to cognitive functional fixedness, prevents agents from restructuring their approach, leading them to pursue unworkable solutions.\nL17: To address this, we propose PPoGA (Predictive Plan-on-Graph with Action), a novel KGQA framework inspired by human cognitive control and problem-solving. PPoGA incorporates a Planner-Executor architecture to separate high-level strategy from low-level execution and leverages a Predictive Processing mechanism to anticipate outcomes.\nL18: The core innovation of our work is a self-correction mechanism that empowers the agent to perform not only Path Correction for local execution errors but also Plan Correction by identifying, discarding, and reformulating the entire plan when it proves ineffective. We conduct extensive experiments on three challenging multi-hop KGQA benchmarks: GrailQA, CWQ, and WebQSP. The results demonstrate that PPoGA achieves state-of-the-art performance, significantly outperforming existing methods.\nL19: Our work highlights the critical importance of metacognitive abilities like problem restructuring for building more robust and flexible AI reasoning systems.\nL20: Subjects:  | Computation and Language (cs.CL); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Information Retrieval (cs.IR)\nL21: Cite as:  | cite10†arXiv:2602.00007 [cs.CL]\nL22:    | (or cite11†arXiv:2602.00007v1 [cs.CL] for this version)\nL23:    | cite12†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2602.00007†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL24: ## Submission history\nL25: \nL26: From: Mingyu Jeon [cite13†view email ]\nL27: [v1] Sat, 22 Nov 2025 10:19:36 UTC (835 KB)\nL28: \nL29: Full-text links:\nL30: \nL31: ## Access Paper:\nL32: \nL33: View a PDF of the paper titled PPoGA: Predictive Plan-on-Graph with Action for Knowledge Graph Question Answering, by MinGyu Jeon and 2 other authors\nL34: \nL35:   * cite8†View PDF L36:   * cite9†HTML (experimental) L37:   * cite14†TeX Source L38: \nL39: cite15†view license L40: ### Current browse context:\nL41: \nL42: cs.CL\nL43: \nL44: cite16†< prev |   cite17†next > L45: \nL46: cite18†new | cite19†recent | cite20†2026-02 L47: \nL48: Change to browse by:\nL49: \nL50: cite21†cs L51: cite22†cs.AI L52: cite23†cs.IR L53: \nL54: ### References & Citations\nL55: \nL56:   * cite24†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L57:   * cite25†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L58:   * cite26†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L59: \nL60: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL61: \nL62: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL63: \nL64: [Button: ×]\nL65: \nL66: loading...\nL67: \nL68: Data provided by:\nL69: \nL70: ### Bookmark\nL71: \nL72: [Input] Bibliographic Tools\nL73: # Bibliographic and Citation Tools\nL74: \nL75: [Input] Bibliographic Explorer Toggle\nL76: \nL77: Bibliographic Explorer (cite27†What is the Explorer?†info.arxiv.org )\nL78: \nL79: [Input] Connected Papers Toggle\nL80: \nL81: Connected Papers (cite28†What is Co